{{Commonscat|Snickers}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Coined in 1930, for a favorite horse owned by the [[w:Mars, Incorporated|Mars]] family.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsnɪkəz/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsnɪkəɹz/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|Snickers|pl2qual=rare|+}}

# A [[chocolate bar]] produced by the [[w:Mars, Incorporated|Mars]] company, with a [[peanut]], [[caramel]] and [[nougat]] filling.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul D. Lehrman|title=Advent and departure: A day at the auction|editor=Richard M. Gaines|journal=[[w:The Phoenix (newspaper)|The Boston Phoenix]]|volume=XI|issue=21|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Phoenix Media/Communications Group|Media Communications Corporation]]|date=25 May 1982|section=Home Entertainment supplement|page=11|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_boston-phoenix_1982-05-25_11_21/page/n114/mode/1up|column=1|issn=0163-3015|passage=Over in a corner of the building, amid the packing crates and pallets, the snack bar is doing a land-office business. Burgers, hot dogs, potato chips, '''Snickerses''', some fresh fruit and some tired-looking pastries, soda, and very respectable coffee are being dispensed by a hurried man named Willie and his three female assistants.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1987|author=Jo Ann Algermissen|title=Couple Diamonds|page=78|passage=From his expression, it seemed that the only thing of importance taking place in the room was his deciding between a '''Snickers''' and a Babe Ruth!}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Page McBrier|title=The Press Mess|page=23|passage="Yep," said Robin, reaching into the cooler for a diet soda and a '''Snickers'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Michele Slung; Roland Hartman|chapter=Preface|title=Murder for Halloween: Tales of Suspense|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Mysterious Press|year=1994|page=xiv|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/murderforhallowe00mich/page/n17/mode/1up|isbn=0-89296-581-9|passage=Yet, though Halloween has long been seen as a time for mild pranks, along with usually unverifiable eerie activity, the dread it inspires seems to us to be more mock than real. Not only are those caramel apples and miniature '''Snickerses''' positively delicious but, for many of us, so is the contemplation of those awful things briefly going “bump!” in the night beyond our windows—that is, as long as we’re safe inside.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2001|author=Grant Guimont|title=A Carousel of Sorts: The Cynic's Secret Guide to Ultimate Happiness|page=269|passage=He saw humans feed them something called a '''Snickers''', and he could feel the pain of those same deer as their stomachs twisted and contorted, but they came back for more of these '''Snickers'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2004|author=Dorothy Koomson|title=The Chocolate Run|page=272|passage=‘…I suppose it’s a bit like '''Snickers''' always being Marathon in my eyes. In about ten years only old people like me will remember it.’<br/> ’No!’ he protested. ‘It’ll never die. In the minds of right-thinking people it’ll always be Marathon. For goodness’ sake, '''Snickers'''! Rhymes with knickers, you don’t want to eat something that does that, do you?’}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2005|author=Ed Broth|title=Stories from a Moron: Real Stories Rejected by Real Magazines|page=135|passage=They just filled up their cars, threw away a Kleenex, grabbed a '''Snickers''', shook out a dress, and washed up.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2007|author=Hugh Prior|title=They Flew So High|page=149|passage=You can hop out of bed, visit the bathroom (and grin in the mirror), make a nice cup of tea, grab a '''Snickers''' and return to bed to daydream the morn away.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Eric B. Wechter|title=Fodor's Pacific Northwest|page=554|passage=Save room for such desserts as '''Snickers''' pie or tiramisu for two.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Cheryl Cory|chapter=1|title=Must’ve Done Something Good|edition=2nd|location=Worcester, Mass.|publisher=Thirteen Hundred Media|year=2008|year_published=2009|page=1|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/mustvedonesometh0000cory/page/1/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4495-7820-6|passage=“Sylvie, you realize we’ll be eating lunch in an hour, right?” This was my older sister Kate, seated to my left, on the aisle. / “Yeah, if it was in an hour and a half, she would’ve gotten ''two'' '''Snickers'''.” This was my younger sister Meg, who always seemed to get the window seat. / “You mean two '''''Snickerses''',''” I told her. “But here are two ''snickers'', just for you,” and here I clicked my tongue disapprovingly—twice.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Peter Sinn Nachtrieb|title=Bob: A Life in Five Acts|page=24|passage=BOB. Jeanine used to pull out a '''Snickers''' from her purse every time we hit a snag, like when there was four hours of traffic to get to Hoover Dam, when I got a B on a chemistry test, or when we got to the Michelle Kwan Museum and it was closed for renovation. I could really use a '''Snickers''' right now.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Tim Carvell|chapter=October 31|title=w:Planet Tad|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:HarperCollins|Harper]]|year=2012|page=206|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/planettad0000carv/page/206/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-06-193436-0|passage=Sophie just came to the door and said, “Tad? I’m sorry you had a bad time at your party. You can have one of my '''Snickerses''' if you want.” Which was really nice of her. I mean, she’s allergic to peanuts, so I’m not sure why she didn’t let me have all the '''Snickers'''. But still .&nbsp;.&nbsp;. it was nice of her.}}

